Observation: # Efficient Test Interference Detection in C Projects -- Scripts

## Setup

### Dependencies

* Podman, tested with 3.4.4
* Standard Linux utilities (sed, awk, etc.), tested with Ubuntu 22.04.4 LTS
* GCC or any other compatible C compiler, tested with GCC 11.4.0

Please note that while these tools may be available for MacOS, their configuration and operation differs from Linux.
In particular [podman run](https://docs.podman.io/en/latest/markdown/podman-run.1.html) lists many restrictions for command line options when running the remote client (which is used on Mac and Windows systems, unless run via WSL on the latter).
A workaround to run our artifact on these systems is to run it inside a Linux virtual machine.

### Installation

* Extract all files into an empty directory (preferably on fast storage) and make sure all scripts are executable (`chmod +x **/*.sh`). `container/gcc` is a wrapper script for `gcc` that adds coverage measurement, but only works if also made executable: `chmod +x container/gcc`. 
* Make sure that (empty) folders called `results_export` and `data` exist. 

Then, follow the steps below.
Don't feel intimidated by the large amount of documented commands in the Usage section -- you may want to jump to the section `Exemplary Usage to Test esnacc-ng` for a short and simple list of commands which can help you quickly start gathering some data, as long as the build process of the project of your choice isn't finicky!

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Usage

### Building Containers for Projects
    ./buildContainer.sh <project name> <git uri>
All Containers are tagged as `autoflakies:<project name>`. Any whitespace in a project name is replaced with an underscore.

**Caution**: If the build succeeds, the tests are executed in default configuration by the script. The results are saved in the folder `results_export/<project name>` with the hash `dd29ecf524b030a65261e3059c48ab9e1ecb2585`. (See "Running Tests" below.)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Patching Non-Building Projects
If a build fails, it is possible to automatically apply patches and hotfix commands during building and testing. Just add the following files into a folder with the name `project name` in the `patches` folder:

- `apt_dependencies`
  Any apt packages in the Debian Testing repository that should be installed prior to building the project, delimited by spaces
  **Caution**: Only packages in the default repositories can be installed this way. Installing packages from a user-repository is possible by directly adding the relevant `apt` commands as a `pre_build_command`
  
- `build_command`
  Any sequence of commands that should be executed *instead* of the default build heuristic (defined in `container/buildAutomakeProject.sh`)
  
- `pre_build_command`
  Any sequence of commands that should be executed *before* the build process (calls to `./configure` and `make`), but after the default build heuristic.
  **Caution**: Will not be executed if `build_command` is defined, as this replaces the whole build script
  
- `pre_test_command`
  Any sequence of commands that should be executed *before* running the tests (call to `make check`)
  
- `*.patch`
  Any file ending with `.patch` will be applied to the project at the beginning

**Caution**: The `*_command` files are *not* scripts, do *not* start them with `#!`. The content will be executed just like if the commands were entered on the (bash) shell. Each command must thus be terminated with `;`

**Example**: If the package `guile-3.0-dev` is required for the project `libmatheval`, and this git repository is located at `~/autoflakies/`, the `apt_dependencies` files must be located at `~/autoflakies/patches/libmatheval/apt_dependencies`

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Running Tests
To manually run a certain combination of tests:

    ./runTest.sh <project name> <tests>
   `tests` can include multiple tests, delimited by a space. The results will be located in the `results_export/<project name>/` folder. Every run of tests or test combinations has an associated hash, calculated from the `tests` string via SHA-1. If the `tests` string is empty, all tests will be executed in the default configuration / order.

**Caution**: Thus the hash `dd29ecf524b030a65261e3059c48ab9e1ecb2585` (SHA-1 hash of an empty string) is more or less a "magic number" for the execution of all tests in default order. The results of this run can be used to get a list of all available tests.

Each tests provides the following results:

- `<hash>-environment`
  This file contains the environment at the start of the test

- `<hash>-environment.diff` (*optional*)
  If the environment is changed after the execution of the test, this diff file is created. Use the `diff` tool and the `<hash>-environment` file to get the environment after the test.

- `<hash>-testlog`
  A log of the test run, containing all information printed during the test run

- `<hash>-result.csv`
  A csv file containing the results of the tests.
  The *first* column contains the name of the test, that is used to call and execute it.
  Some tests that use Autotest (detectable by `*.at` files in the `tests/` folder of the project under test) additonally require the file in which the test is defined. If required, this information is written to the *second* column.
  **Caution**: The paper does not take a look at Projects which use Autotest. You can safely ignore the second and third column.
  The *last* column contains the result of the test, which most of the time is either PASS, SKIP or FAIL.

- `<hash>-files.txz`
  An archive of the test artifacts, including coverage data, `*.trs` result files and any `*.log` log files.
  
- `<hash>-files`
  A list of files changed during the execution of the container, based on reports of OverlayFS / `podman diff`.

- `<hash>-syscalls/trace.*`
  In the `<hash>-syscalls` folder, *all* syscalls called during test execution are saved. This allows analysis of existing test runs, without the need to rerun it.
  **Caution**: These folders can fill up *very* fast, as the files are saved without compression. Keep an eye on the free disk space. You may use `./runTestAutoclean.sh`, which is used the same way as `./runTest.sh` is, but automatically compresses this folder

Additionally, for each project, two additional files are created / modified:

- `<project name>-index.csv`
  A list with hash values in the *first* column and the actual test strings in the *second* column, allowing a quick assignment of results to the relevant `tests` string.

- `commit_sha`
  The commit hash of the HEAD, when the project was first built. Useful to recreate containers with identical versions of the project under test.
  **Caution**: As the Dockerfile uses the current Debian Stable image and current versions of any dependencies installed by APT, rebuilding the container is *not* guaranteed to result in an identical container - only that the same git commit will be used.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Running All Tests of a Project Independently
To run each Test of a project independently:

    ./runAllTests.sh <project name>

This script automatically reads all available tests from the `dd29ecf524b030a65261e3059c48ab9e1ecb2585-results.csv` file and executes each test one time.

This produces the same artifacts per test as manually running each test with `runTest.sh`.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Getting All Accessed Ressources, Generating Permutations And Filtering
If your program also uses sockets (or you don't know if it does) you have to also run

    ./getSyscallsSockets.sh <project name>

*before* running `fast_syscall_extraction`. The script generates pseudo "open" calls for every `bind` to a socket, which are parsed by `fast_syscall_extraction`.

Running `./getSyscallsSockets.sh` will also create the following data in the `data` directory

- `<project>-socket-per-test.csv`
  A list of all network sockets used for listening

To generate a list(s) of potential permutations:

    ./fast_syscall_extraction <project name>

This program (we provide source code along with a x86-64-Linux compatible binary) parses each `trace.*` file in each `<hash>-syslog/` folder and creates lists of files that were accessed with either pure read access (saved to `<hash>-read-fast`) or potential write access (saved to `<hash>-write-fast` for changes to files which were reported as changed by OverlayFS at the end of the test, `<hash>-write-unchanged-fast` for any other files).
Following this, it generates two permutation lists per project, based on the parsed trace files, a `<project name>-permutations-fd-fast.csv` for all permutations required by the FD method and a `<project name>-permutations-fd-ofs-fast.csv` for all permutations required by the (pure, unfiltered) OFS method.
Additionally, it applies the FDF filter to the artifacts generated, generating two additional permutation lists per project, a `<project name>-permutations-fdf-fast.csv` for all permutations required by the FDF method and a `<project name>-permutations-fd-ofs-fdf-fast.csv` for all permutations required by the OFSFDF method.

**Caution**: This program requires write access to the `results_export` folder. By default, Podman executes the container as root. If this script fails due to missing write access, either the container must be executed as the current user or the permissions of the `results_export` folder must be changed (recursively)

**Caution**: As `make check` creates build artifacts and access most sources with write access, it is quite likely that there are many files each test accesses with write access. As a countermeasure, one can ignore such build artifacts by removing all entries from the `<project name>-permutations.csv` file that are obviously part of the build process, like `*.h`, `*.c`, `*.at` and `Makefile` files.

**Caution**: The resulting potential-interference lists are filtered in such a way that each test pair will only appear once, allowing them to be directly used to execute the permutations.

**Note**: Please consult the paper for proper explanations of the different analyses in this step.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Running Tests in Permutation

To execute all the permutations defined in one of the generated permutation lists:

    ./runPermutatedTests.sh <project name> [<type>]
This script executes the permutations defined in the `<project name>-permutations-final*.csv` permutation lists created with `fast_syscall_extraction`. If no type is specified, the FD permutation list (`<project name>-permutations-fd.csv`) is executed. To execute the OFSFDF method, specify `fd-ofs-fdf` (careful, *not* `ofs-fdf`). Each test that accesses a file will be executed *after* each test that accesses a file *with write access*. Thus, any order-dependency should change the result of the tests.

The result look-up for permutations is a two-step process:

1. `<project-name>-index.csv` contains the hash sum for each executed test pair.
2. Using the hash sum, the result files (most notably `<hash>-result.csv`) can be found.

**Caution**: `<type>` is used unescaped in file system operations. Directory traversal is possible. Do not use user generated values, only `fdf`, `ofs` or `ofs-fdf`.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Benchmark Serial vs. Parallel Test Suite Execution
To benchmark both executing the test suite in series and parallel (via `make -jN check`):

    ./runBenchmarkForProject.sh <project name>
This script rebuilds an existing container to enable benchmarking - the new container is tagged as "`<project name>`_benchmark" - and runs the test suite both in series and parallel 50 times. The test data is saved to the `result_export_benchmark` folder, with each test run getting a seperate subfolder.
The core parameters (actual runtime, cputime and used memory) are saved to `benchmark-results.csv`, data about the consistency of the results to `benchmark.log`

**Caution**: The container to rebuilt must already exist or be created via `./buildContainer.sh`

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Benchmark Throttled Serial vs. Parallel Test Suite Execution
To benchmark both executing the test suite serially and in parallel (via `make -jN check`), throttled to 10% of the available CPU power:

    ./runBenchmarkThrottledForProject.sh <project name>
This script works identical to `./runBenchmarkForProject.sh`, but executes the test suites while restricting the amount of available CPU to 10%.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Generate Serial vs. Parallel Coverage Data
To generate coverage data:

    ./runCoverageForProject.sh <project name>
This script works identical to `./runBenchmarkForProject.sh`, but generates coverage data for 10 serial and parallel runs. The coverage data is saved the the `result_export_gcov` folder, with each test run getting a seperate subfolder. The gcov data is saved as `dd29ecf524b030a65261e3059c48ab9e1ecb2585-gcov.txt` (human readable) and `dd29ecf524b030a65261e3059c48ab9e1ecb2585-gcov.json` (machine readable). The raw GCOV output files are saved to the compressed `dd29ecf524b030a65261e3059c48ab9e1ecb2585-files.tgz` file

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Gather Data
To gather data about permutations, coverage and benchmarks:

    ./gatherData.sh <project name>
This script generates various files into the `data` folder:

- `<project>-benchmark-results.csv`
  A list of all benchmark results


